{"term":"cornuto","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man whose wife or partner is sexually unfaithful; etymologically from Latin for 'horned,' invoking the metaphor of shame-horns placed on the deceived partner.","english_term":"cuckold","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Dual use: literal sexual meaning and a general-purpose insult (analogous to English 'bastard') fully detached from its sexual origin; among the most culturally loaded sexual terms in Italian; the associated hand gesture extends the metaphor into gesture culture."}
{"term":"mettere le corna","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":52,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian idiom meaning to be sexually unfaithful to one's partner; literally 'to put horns on someone,' naming the act from the cheater's active perspective.","english_term":"to cuckold (someone)","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Framed from the cheater's side; forms a semantic triplet with 'portare le corna' (victim's state) and 'fare le corna' (the mocking gesture); the horn metaphor derives from cuckoo bird behavior and Roman-era folklore."}
{"term":"portare le corna","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Italian idiom meaning to be the victim of a partner's infidelity; literally 'to carry the horns,' naming the cuckolded person's shameful condition.","english_term":"to be cuckolded","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Passive/victim perspective counterpart to 'mettere le corna'; the metaphor of carrying an unwanted burden (horns) maps cleanly onto the English passive construction 'to wear the horns.'"}
{"term":"fare le corna","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":35,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To perform the hand gesture with index and pinky fingers extended toward someone, either to mock them as a cornuto or—in a completely unrelated use—to ward off the evil eye (malocchio).","english_term":"to flash the cuckold horns","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The sexual-shaming and apotropaic meanings coexist so thoroughly that ambiguity is pervasive; directed at a person it typically means cuckold-shaming, directed outward it means evil-eye protection; the overlap is culturally specific and has no clean English parallel."}
{"term":"onore","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_honor","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual honor; specifically, a woman's (and by extension her family's) reputation for sexual purity, functioning as a jealously guarded form of social capital in Mediterranean honor-shame culture.","english_term":"(sexual) honor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The ordinary Italian word for 'honor' carries a highly specific sexual valence in the honor-shame framework that the English gloss understates; a woman's onore is simultaneously her own and her family's to protect or destroy, with material social consequences."}
{"term":"disonore","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonor","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":8,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual dishonor; the state of having violated or lost sexual honor, whether through premarital sex, rape, adultery, or perceived sexual looseness, with attendant social ostracism.","english_term":"(sexual) dishonor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Historically triggered concrete consequences—family violence, forced marriage, exile—far beyond what the English gloss implies; crucially, a woman could acquire disonore through rape with no agency on her part."}
{"term":"vergogna","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":8,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual shame; the acute social and emotional distress experienced when one has violated or is perceived to have violated the norms of sexual propriety in an honor-shame culture.","english_term":"(sexual) shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The everyday Italian word for 'shame'; in the honor-shame framework it is the internal counterpart to the external sanctions of slut-shaming; 'far vergogna' (to bring shame) is a common construction applied to women's sexual behavior."}
{"term":"svergognato","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":38,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A shameless person who behaves without regard for sexual propriety; one who has lost or deliberately abandoned vergogna, especially in a sexual context.","english_term":"shameless (person)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Adjective or noun; feminine form 'svergognata' carries heavier sexual connotation when applied to women due to the asymmetric honor-shame framework; roughly equivalent to archaic English 'brazen hussy' when used of a woman."}
{"term":"disonorata","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonor","concept":"slut_shame","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A dishonored woman; a female who has lost her sexual honor through premarital sex, rape, adultery, or perceived sexual looseness and is consequently treated as a social outcast.","english_term":"dishonored woman","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Crucially, a woman could be 'disonorata' through rape with no volitional sexual act on her part, exposing the victim-blaming core of the concept; historically used to justify family violence ('lavare l'onore col sangue')."}
{"term":"macchia sull'onore","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_dishonor","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":10,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A stain on one's honor; the permanent, indelible mark of sexual disgrace left on an individual and their family by any perceived sexual transgression.","english_term":"stain on one's honor","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The stain metaphor implies irreversibility: unlike a temporary failure, a macchia sull'onore cannot be washed away, only revenged; this framing historically justified honour killings as acts of 'cleansing.'"}
{"term":"onta","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"sexual_shame","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":8,"technical":18,"recognition_pct_current":62,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An archaic and literary Italian word for deep shame, infamy, or dishonor, especially of a sexual nature; more severe and formal in register than vergogna.","english_term":"infamy / sexual disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymologically related to Latin 'ignominia'; retained today in elevated or deliberately archaizing register; functions as an intensified and publicly visible vergogna implying irremediable social infamy rather than private shame."}
{"term":"gelosia","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"jealousy","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":8,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual jealousy; the possessive emotional response to a partner's real or suspected infidelity, which in the Italian honor-shame framework functions simultaneously as an emotion and as a social obligation for men.","english_term":"(sexual) jealousy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The ordinary Italian word for jealousy; in honor-shame culture a man who fails to exhibit gelosia is suspected of being complicit in his own cuckolding; this social obligation of jealousy is the emotional engine of the cornuto complex."}
{"term":"tradimento","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"infidelity","concept":"love_affair","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual betrayal or infidelity within a committed relationship; encompasses both the act of infidelity and the moral violation of the trust and honor it represents.","english_term":"betrayal / infidelity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The ordinary Italian word for 'betrayal'; in sexual discourse it specifically denotes partner infidelity; carries heavier moral weight than English 'cheating' because it explicitly frames infidelity as a breach of honor-code obligation, not merely a personal failing."}
{"term":"adulterio","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":18,"technical":28,"recognition_pct_current":96,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Adultery; extramarital sexual intercourse committed by a married person, historically a criminal offense in Italy until 1969 and still carrying strong moral weight in honor-shame culture.","english_term":"adultery","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More specific than fornication (requires at least one party to be married); a criminal offense in Italy until 1969 under a gender-asymmetric law that punished wives but not husbands equally, reflecting the honor-shame framework's legal institutionalization."}
{"term":"adultera","lang":"it","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":22,"technical":12,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An adulteress; a woman who commits adultery, typically carrying severe moral condemnation and social consequences within the Italian honor-shame framework.","english_term":"adulteress","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Feminine form of 'adultero'; the asymmetric application of shame means 'adultera' historically triggered far more severe social and legal consequences for women than the masculine form did for men under the same legal code."}
